lesson: 147
title: Sum and multiple: two amounts from their total and «times as many»
level: primary-3
stars: 1
explain: If one amount is 3 times another, draw the smaller as 1 part and the bigger as 3 parts: together they are 1 + 3 = 4 equal parts.
explain: Divide the total by the number of parts to get one part, the smaller amount; multiply to get the bigger.
explain: Two boxes hold 48 apples, the first 3 times as many as the second: 48 ÷ 4 = 12 in the second, 12 × 3 = 36 in the first.
vars: a=name b=name t=thing x=4..120 m=2..6
let: s = x * (m + 1)
need: s <= 600
answer: m * x
exercise: Two boxes hold {s} {t} together. The first box holds {m} times as many {t} as the second. How many {t} are in the first box?
exercise: {a} and {b} have {s} {t} in all. {a} has {m} times as many {t} as {b}. How many {t} does {a} have?
exercise: A ribbon {s} cm long is cut into two pieces. One piece is {m} times as long as the other. How long is the longer piece?
exercise: There are {s} children in a club, and there are {m} times as many girls as boys. How many girls are in the club?
exercise: Two numbers add up to {s}. The bigger number is {m} times the smaller. What is the bigger number?
exercise: A farmer has {s} animals, only cows and sheep. He has {m} times as many sheep as cows. How many sheep does he have?
test: {a}'s pile of {t} is {m} times as big as {b}'s pile, and the two piles have {s} {t} between them. How many {t} are in {a}'s pile?
test: A shop sold {s} kg of fruit on Monday and Tuesday. On Tuesday it sold {m} times as much as on Monday. How many kilograms did it sell on Tuesday?
test: If {a} put all {a.his} {t} together with {b}'s, there would be {s}. {a} owns {m} times the number that {b} owns. How many {t} belong to {a}?
test: A jar of juice and a bottle of water hold {s} cl altogether. The jar holds {m} times as much as the bottle. How many centilitres does the jar hold?
exercise: {a} and {b} collected {s} {t} altogether. {a} collected {m.words} times as many as {b}. How many {t} did {a} collect?
exercise: How many {t} does {a} have, if {a.he} has {m} times as many as {b} and they have {s} {t} combined?
exercise: A school in Ohio has {s} pupils in two buildings. The new building has {m} times as many pupils as the old one. How many pupils are in the new building?
exercise: Two piles of logs on a farm in Maine weigh {s} kg in total. The big pile weighs {m} times as much as the small one. How many kilograms does the big pile weigh?
exercise: {a} and {b} share {s} dollars so that {a} gets {m} times as much as {b}. How many dollars does {a} get?
exercise: A bookcase in a library in Leeds holds {s} books on its two shelves. The bottom shelf has {m.words} times as many books as the top shelf. How many books are on the bottom shelf?
exercise: On a farm in New South Wales there are {s} sheep and cows together, and there are {m} times as many sheep as cows. How many sheep are there?
exercise: The sum of two numbers is {s}, and one of them is {m} times the other. Find the larger number.
test: {b} has {s} {t} together with {a}, and {a}'s share is {m} times {b}'s. How many of the {t} are {a}'s?
test: In a village in Ireland, {s} people live on two streets. One street has {m} times as many people as the other. How many people live on the bigger street?
test: A train has {s} passengers in two carriages, the first carrying {m} times as many as the second. How many passengers are in the first carriage?
test: Two tanks in a fish shop in Toronto hold {s} litres of water between them. The big tank holds {m.words} times as much as the small one. How many litres are in the big tank?
test: How large is the bigger of two parts of {s}, if it is {m} times the smaller part?
test: A plank {s} cm long is sawn in two. The long piece is {m} times the length of the short piece. How many centimetres long is the long piece?
exercise: 2 boxes, {s} {t} altogether, big one's got {m} times the small one. how many in the big one
exercise: {a} and {b} got {s} {t} between em, {a} has {m+1} times — no wait, {m} times what {b} has. how many {a} got
exercise: my mate says {a} got {x}. nah that's {b}'s lot innit. {s} {t} total, {a} has {m} times what {b} has — how many for {a}
exercise: {s} {t} in 2 bags, one bag's got {m} times the other, oh and it took {a} 10 minutes to count em. how many in the bigger bag
test: {a}'s pile is {m} times {b}'s, {s} {t} both piles together. how big's {a}'s pile
test: me n my sis got {s} {t}, she got {m} times as many as me. how many she got
test: {a} and {b}, {s+4} {t} together, no wait {s}. {a} got {m} times as many as {b}. {a}'s got how many?
exam: It is given that two containers hold, in aggregate, {s} {t}, and that the quantity in the first container is {m.words} times that in the second. Determine the number of {t} contained in the first container.
exam: Whereas {a} and {b} possess {s} {t} in total, and the holding of {a} is {m} times that of {b}, ascertain the number of {t} in the possession of {a}.
exam: Ascertain the larger of two quantities of {t} whose sum is {s} and of which the larger is {m.words} times the smaller.
exam: A collection of {s} {t} is apportioned between {a} and {b} in such a manner that {b} receives {m} times as many as {a}. State the number of {t} hereinafter allotted to {b}.
exercise: When Gran passed away, she left {s} dollars to share between {a} and {b}. {a}, who looked after her, gets {m} times as many dollars as {b}. How many dollars does {a} get?
exercise: {a} was being creative with the truth: {a.he} said {a.he} and {b} had the same number of {t}. Really, of their {s} {t}, {a} has {m} times as many as {b}. How many {t} does {a} have?
test: {b}'s telling porkies again, "we split 'em fair". fair my foot. {s} {t} between {a} and {b}, and {a} grabbed {m} times what {b} got. how many did that greedy so-and-so take
